            "text": "**Pedophilia** is defined in clinical psychology and psychiatry as a psychiatric condition characterized by intense, recurring sexual urges, fantasies, or behaviors involving prepubescent children (generally children aged 13 or younger). An individual with this condition is clinically referred to as having a pedophilic interest or, if specific diagnostic criteria are met, **pedophilic disorder**.\n\n### Clinical Definition and Criteria\n\nAccording to the *Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders* (DSM-5), published by the American Psychiatric Association, a diagnosis of **pedophilic disorder** requires:\n\n1. **Duration:** The individual has experienced intense, recurrent sexual arousal from prepubescent children over a period of at least six months.\n2. **Age requirement of the individual:** The person must be at least 16 years old and at least five years older than the prepubescent child or children.\n3. **Impairment or Action:** The individual has either acted on these urges, or the urges cause clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.\n\n### Distinction Between Orientation/Condition and Behavior\n\n* **Pedophilia (Interest/Disorder):** Refers to the psychological interest or arousal pattern.\n* **Child Sexual Exploitation and Abuse (CSEA):** Refers to the illegal and harmful *actions* or behaviors taken against a child, including physical abuse or the possession/distribution of illicit material. \n\nWhile individuals who commit sexual offenses against prepubescent children often have pedophilic interests, not all child sex offenders fit the clinical definition of pedophilia (offenses can be driven by other factors, such as opportunism, power, or antisocial traits). Conversely, an individual may experience pedophilic urges but actively choose never to act on them and seek mental health treatment.\n\n### Treatment and Prevention\n\nMental health professionals and international prevention organizations focus on providing psychological support to help individuals manage these impulses and prevent harm to children:\n\n* **Psychotherapy:** Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) is frequently used to help individuals recognize triggers, manage distress, regulate impulses, and establish strict boundaries to prevent any harmful behavior.\n* **Pharmacological Interventions:** In some severe cases, medications that reduce libido or obsessive thoughts (such as anti-androgens or SSRIs) may be used under strict medical supervision.\n* **Confidential Help Lines:** Several international public health initiatives (such as *Stop It Now!* in the US/UK and *Kein Täter werden* in Germany) provide confidential, non-judgmental resources for individuals experiencing unwanted attractions, aiming to intervene and prevent child sexual abuse before it occurs.",
